D. SYNTHESIS
============================================================================================
  1. Local endpoint repair is not the same as a counterexample.
     The cheapest window covers leave divisor rows unpaid; paying them
     in deterministic completions gives positive-gap systems in all
     three cases.
  2. The minimum-window family audit isolates the obstruction:
     n=14 can pay all coarse rows at minimum local size only in gcd-2
     even covers; n=15 minimum local covers always leave at least two
     coarse rows unpaid; n=16 has a unique minimum local cover, also
     gcd-2 and still missing rows 7,12,14.
  3. n=15 behaves like a mixed odd-prime product building, not like the
     scalar n=14 moat or the pure dyadic n=16 ladder.  Its best quotient
     ladder product here is the 3-branch value 7/11, while the 5-branch
     has smaller visible gap but larger endpoint debt.
  4. A plausible proof target is now a Hall/dual certificate: local
     gate-endpoint rows plus coarse divisor rows cannot be paid by
     n-1 primitive columns without leaving either positive gap or
     unprotected endpoint debt.
